Part B — Deterministic Reflection Agent
1. Overview

For Part B, I implemented a command-line agent that executes the reflection tree designed in Part A.

The goal was to translate a structured psychological model into a working, deterministic system—one that guides users through reflection without relying on any LLM at runtime.

The agent walks the user through three axes:

Agency (Locus): how much control they exercised
Contribution (Orientation): what they gave vs expected
Perspective (Radius): how widely they considered others

The system guarantees deterministic execution:
the same inputs always produce the same path and the same output, ensuring consistency, auditability, and trust.

2. Architecture
2.1 Separation of Data and Logic

All conversational intelligence is encoded in the tree (.tsv file), not in the code.

The agent acts purely as an execution engine:

It reads nodes
Displays content
Routes based on predefined rules

This separation makes the system:

Easy to modify without touching code
Reusable across interfaces (CLI, web, etc.)
Fully transparent in behavior
2.2 Core Components

Tree Loader
Parses the TSV file into structured nodes.

Execution Engine
Walks the tree node-by-node:

question → capture input
decision → evaluate rules and route
reflection/bridge → display and continue

State Manager
Maintains:

User answers (answers[node_id])
Signal counts across axes

Template Renderer
Injects prior answers into reflections:

"You said '{A1_OPEN.answer}'..."
3. Deterministic Branching

Branching is entirely rule-based.

Example:

answer=Productive|Mixed → A1_Q1_HIGH  
answer=Tough|Frustrating → A1_Q1_LOW

At runtime:

The agent reads the previous answer
Matches it against defined rules
Routes to the corresponding node

There is:

No randomness
No inference
No ambiguity

This ensures every path is predictable and debuggable.

4. Signal-Based Reasoning

Instead of using opaque scoring models, the system uses explicit signal accumulation.

Each response contributes to an axis:

axis1: internal / external  
axis2: contribution / entitlement  
axis3: self / altro

Signals are tallied during execution:

state.axis1.internal += 1

At key points, the system determines the dominant orientation per axis.

This approach is:

Transparent
Interpretable
Easy to audit

At any point, the user’s journey can be reconstructed from stored answers and signals.

5. Psychological Mapping

Each axis directly operationalizes established behavioral frameworks:

Agency (Locus of Control):
Questions distinguish between reaction-based vs action-based interpretations of events.
Contribution (Entitlement vs Citizenship):
Prompts surface whether focus is on what was given vs what was expected.
Perspective (Self vs Others):
Questions expand the frame from individual experience to team and system impact.

Rather than referencing theory abstractly, these constructs are encoded into forced-choice questions, making them observable and measurable.

6. Branching Strategy

The tree is designed using a three-stage structure:

Early divergence → captures meaningful mindset differences
Mid-stage compression → avoids combinatorial explosion
Late synthesis → produces clear, interpretable reflections

This is a deliberate trade-off:

Instead of modeling every possible path, the system groups patterns using signals and resolves them at the axis level.

This keeps the tree:

Scalable
Maintainable
Psychologically meaningful
7. Reflection Design

Reflections are carefully written to:

Surface awareness without judgment
Avoid moralizing or labeling
Encourage small shifts in perspective

They follow principles from motivational interviewing:

The system does not tell the user what they are—it helps them notice patterns in how they showed up.

8. Personalization Without Variability

Personalization is achieved through:

1. Answer Interpolation

"You described your day as '{A1_OPEN.answer}'..."

2. Signal-Based Framing

"You showed signs of ownership..."

This creates relevance without sacrificing determinism.

9. Sample Paths
Persona 1 — Reactive / Entitled / Self-Focused
Describes day as tough
Attributes difficulty externally
Focuses on unmet expectations
Frames challenges individually

Outcome:
The system highlights:

overlooked agency
limited contribution
narrow perspective

The tone remains non-judgmental, focusing on awareness rather than correction.

Persona 2 — Proactive / Contributing / Other-Oriented
Describes day as productive
Attributes outcomes to own actions
Contributes beyond role
Considers team or customer impact

Outcome:
The system reinforces:

ownership
contribution
outward perspective

Encouraging consistency rather than escalation.

10. User Experience Considerations

The interaction is intentionally lightweight:

~2–3 minutes to complete
Minimal cognitive load
Clear, structured progression

This aligns with real-world usage—end-of-day reflection when users are mentally fatigued.

11. Limitations
Reflections are generalized at the axis level
No longitudinal tracking across days
Limited depth in summary variation

These are conscious trade-offs to maintain simplicity and determinism.

12. Future Improvements
Multi-day tracking with trend visualization
UI layer with axis-based dashboards
More granular reflection categories
Adaptive depth (quick vs deep reflection modes)
Integration into broader coaching or management systems
13. Conclusion

This implementation demonstrates that meaningful behavioral reflection can be achieved through structured, deterministic systems.

Instead of relying on probabilistic AI:

The questions drive insight
The structure ensures relevance
The consistency builds trust

This aligns with a core design principle:

Intelligence should be encoded in the system’s structure—not inferred at runtime.
